You are reviewing the author check of the Goodreads re-import on The Greatest Books, using the admin MCP server at <%= mcp_url %>. The check compared each book's credited authors with the author its readers' Goodreads rows name, and recorded a reimport_author verdict where they seemed to disagree. The queue holds <%= pending_authors %> proposed reimport_author verdicts. Work through all of them.

Use list_repair_verdicts with kind=reimport_author to list them (status defaults to proposed). Page through the whole list.

What a verdict says (get_repair_verdict):

- `books` is the book, with the authors it credits now.
- `payload.row_author_names` is what the rows say: the author most of the book's Goodreads editions name first, then any co-author the AI confirmed.
- `payload.after` maps each of those names to the author the verdict would credit: an existing author's id, or null for a new author it would create. `authors` lists the existing ones.
- `payload.ai` holds the AI's matches and what the author finder said about each name.
- Approving makes the next books:goodreads_reimport:apply replace the book's credits with exactly `after`, in that order, creating an author for every null. Rejecting leaves the book as it is.

For each verdict, decide whether the book's current credit is wrong. Use the book's title and year, the author records (get_admin_author, search_admin_authors) and your own web search.

- **The current credit is right: reject it** with reject_repair_verdict. Most verdicts will be this. The rows' name is often a variant of the same person: a misspelling ("Louis May Alcott"), a reversed or slug form ("Wharton Edith", "heller-joseph"), a transliteration or translation ("Fedor Dostoyevsky", "丹尼尔·笛福"), a pen name of the same person, or the translator, narrator, illustrator or editor of one edition credited first. None of these is a reason to change a book's author.
- **Never create an author from a misspelling or a variant.** If `after` has null for a name that is a variant of an author who already exists, the verdict is wrong.
- **Never credit a duplicate author record.** If `after` points at a second record of the book's own author (the same person under another spelling), the verdict is wrong: reject it and note both author ids in your report.
- **"Unknown", "Anonymous", "Various" and place-named authors (for example "China") are deliberate placeholders.** Never replace one with a person.
- **The current credit is wrong and `after` is exactly right: approve** with approve_repair_verdict. Exactly right means the right people, each an existing record when one exists, no duplicates, and no co-author of the book left out.
- **The current credit is wrong but `after` is not exactly right:** reject the verdict, then fix the book yourself. Find the right authors with search_admin_authors; add them with add_book_author, remove the wrong one with remove_book_author, and set the order with update_book_author (position 1 is the main author). Create an author with create_admin_author only when the person has no record, passing their wikidata_qid when you can find it. A namesake (a different person with the same name) is a common cause: check birth and death years and the books each record holds.

Use bulk_approve_repair_verdicts only for verdicts you have each read with get_repair_verdict. Do not approve a whole page unread.

Approving does not change anything at once. The next books:goodreads_reimport:apply run acts on it, so treat each answer as final.

You are authorised to act without asking me. If you genuinely cannot tell, skip the verdict and say why.

When you finish, report what you did with each verdict: its id, the book, what you did (approved, rejected, rejected and fixed by hand, skipped) and one line of reasoning. List any duplicate author records you found, with both ids.
